! EXIC (EXECUTE INDIRECT AND COUNT): ITS TWO ADDRESS RESTRICTIONS, and
! the DATA STORE alarm on its own effective address.
!
! THREE RULES, all read first-hand at 300 dpi for this program.
!
! 1. THE PSEUDO-INSTRUCTION COUNTER MAY NOT LIVE BELOW 16.  Reference
!    Manual 22-6530-2 printed p.50 (PDF 53): "Pseudo-instruction
!    counters may occupy any of the index registers as well as main core
!    storage locations but cannot occupy locations addressed between 0
!    and 15."
!
!    THAT SENTENCE FINISHES AT THE TOP OF THE NEXT COLUMN, and the page
!    sets a trap for a skimming eye: the right column of PDF p.53 OPENS
!    with "15.  Subject instructions of EXECUTE INDIRECT AND COUNT may
!    occupy any available locations with addresses 32.00 and greater."
!    The "15." is the TAIL of the left column's sentence, not a list
!    number, and the two halves are DIFFERENT rules with DIFFERENT
!    boundaries.  Verified here by reading the page, not the text layer.
!
! 2. THE SUBJECT INSTRUCTION MUST LIE AT 32.00 OR ABOVE -- the other
!    half of that same sentence, and the one EX does NOT share: the
!    paragraph above it says "Subject instructions of EXECUTE may occupy
!    any addressable location."
!
! 3. THE EXIC'S OWN EFFECTIVE ADDRESS IS A DATA STORE ADDRESS.  Printed
!    p.32 (PDF 35) names this instruction to say so: "Address monitoring
!    for Data Store includes: ... 5. The effective address of EXECUTE
!    INDIRECT AND COUNT."  It is NOT in the Data Fetch list, and the
!    same page says why -- "A fetch-type operation is one in which the
!    contents of an addressed storage location are read out for use but
!    are not subject to change" -- and a pseudo-IC is read out AND
!    changed.  The consequence is stated twice: p.32, "Since this
!    indicator is permanently masked on, storing in core storage is
!    always suppressed, and the operation is always terminated"; p.45
!    item 19, "The operation was suppressed."
!
! WHAT (1) AND (2) DO WHEN VIOLATED comes from printed p.45 item 16 and
! from the deck, which agree: "Address Invalid (AD).  The location of
! the instruction next due for execution is at an address not provided
! in the computer system, or the location specified by the effective
! operand address of the instruction just attempted is either not
! provided in the computer system or INVALID FOR THE SPECIFIED
! OPERATION.  The affected instruction is executed as a NO OPERATION."
! jb_ax5b routine 1262 TEST 3A (pseudo-IC below 15) and TEST 3B
! (subject below 32) each carry three error messages saying exactly
! that: no interrupt, AD set, the instruction NOPed.  Both are
! transcribed for their ENCODING as programs/ax5b_162_test3a.strap and
! ax5b_162_test3b.strap and cannot be run (SSIP, the interrupt table
! and routine 1260's subroutines).  THIS FILE IS THEIR BEHAVIOURAL
! COUNTERPART for the two parts this model has: AD set, and the
! instruction no-opped.  The third, "no interrupt" -- p.50's "at the
! completion, termination, or suppression of an execution operation,
! interruption is always permitted", which overrides the DISABLED state
! -- is NOT implemented and is recorded as such in docs/NEXT.md.
!
! BOTH BOUNDARIES ARE PINNED FROM BOTH SIDES, which is the point of
! blocks A2 and C: a model that wrote "< 32" for the counter would pass
! A and fail A2, and one that wrote "< 16" for the subject would pass C
! and fail B.
!
!   A   counter at WORD 15, the highest illegal one      -> AD, no-op
!   A2  counter at WORD 16 ($X0), the lowest legal one   -> runs
!   B   subject at WORD 31 ($X15), the highest illegal   -> AD, no-op
!   C   subject at WORD 32 (32.00), the lowest legal     -> runs
!   D   counter inside the protected area, enabled       -> DS, and the
!       whole operation suppressed
!
! THE MANUAL AND THE DECK DISAGREE ABOUT BOUNDARY (1) AND THIS PROGRAM
! FOLLOWS THE MANUAL.  1262 TEST 3A's three messages all say "LESS THAN
! 15"; p.50 says "between 0 and 15".  No deck row discriminates -- its
! counter is word 9, illegal under either reading -- so block A uses
! word 15 and asserts it is ILLEGAL, which is the manual's reading and
! the only one the machine's storage map supports: 0-15 are the special
! registers and 16-31 are the index registers, which the same sentence
! expressly ALLOWS.  If a deck row ever turns up with a counter at word
! 15, this block is the one it contradicts.  Recorded in docs/NEXT.md.
!
! ELEVEN INJECTIONS MOVE THIS PROGRAM -- the eight halts below, four of
! them sharing one row -- EACH HALT OBSERVED (not predicted).  Every one was applied to a pristine snapshot of
! bsv/StretchExecute.bsv by an exact-substring substitution that ABORTS
! unless its anchor occurs exactly once, and printed its unified diff
! before the run -- because a `sed` that silently fails to match prints
! ALL TESTS PASSED, which reads exactly like an honest "this does not
! discriminate".  After each run the patch was REVERSED and the file
! compared byte-for-byte with the snapshot.  Reproduce any row with
! `python3 line_addresses.py programs/exic_addr.strap`.
!
!   the counter's low-address guard        -> 7174  007003.00
!     removed (`if (False)`)                     B, $          (A)
!                                                -- AD was not set
!   the counter's guard WIDENED to         -> 7182  007007.00
!     `pw < 17`, so word 16 is illegal          BAD, $         (A2)
!                                                -- AD set where p.50
!                                                   allows a counter
!   the subject's low-address guard        -> 7190  007013.00
!     removed (`if (False)`)                     B, $          (B)
!                                                -- AD was not set
!   the subject's guard WIDENED to         -> 7198  007017.00
!     `< 33`, so 32.00 is illegal               BAD, $         (C)
!   the Data Store check removed           -> 7215  007027.40
!     (`else if (False)`)                        BZB, 6.0, $   (D)
!                                                -- no DS, and the
!                                                   subject ran
!   DS SET BUT NOT TERMINATED -- the        -> 7215  007027.40
!     "set the indicator and carry on"           BZB, 6.0, $   (D)
!     reading, rejected on p.32's "the
!     operation is always terminated"
!                                                AND THE INDICATOR
!                                                VANISHED TOO: a call
!                                                that bails has its
!                                                whole indicator word
!                                                discarded (execute()'s
!                                                tail), so DS never
!                                                reached the register
!                                                and the counter was
!                                                stepped inside the
!                                                protected area.  That
!                                                is a second reason the
!                                                termination belongs
!                                                BEFORE the read.
!   step size always TWO / the step        -> 7202  007021.00
!     clobbering bits 19-63 / no step /          BZXE, $       (C)
!     the counter word executed
!   the subject installed under its OWN    -> 7297  007100.40
!     pc and len                                 WRONGA B, $
!
! FIVE FURTHER INJECTIONS LEAVE THIS PROGRAM GREEN and belong to
! programs/exic_subject.strap: the "always ONE" step (this file has no
! full-length subject), bit 18 of the effective address (no operand here
! has it set), the straddle read, the `under` flag, and the step skipped
! when the subject is itself an execution operation (no subject here is
! one).  Sixteen injections were run across the pair; every one moved at
! least one of the two programs and none survived.
!
! Instructions used: BD, BE, Z, LX, SX, LV, LVI, SV, KV, V+I, EXIC,
! BAD, BADZ, BZXE, BB1, BBZ, BZB, B, BEW -- all implemented.

        SLC, (8)7000.0
START   BD,  $+.32                 ! disabled: blocks A-C are not about
                                   !   address monitoring
!
! ---- A. the counter at WORD 15 -- the highest illegal location ----
        Z,   $X1
        LX,  $X2, PSICA            ! a well-formed counter, aimed at SUBJA
        SX,  $X2, 15.0             ! ... planted in word 15
        EXIC, 15.0                 ! THE TEST
        BADZ, A1                   ! AD must be SET (and is cleared here)
        B,   $                     ! hang: AD was not set
A1      KV,  $X1, ZEROW
        BZXE, $                    ! hang: the subject RAN
        LX,  $X3, 15.0
        SX,  $X3, RESA             ! word 15 must be UNCHANGED: not stepped
!
! ---- A2. the counter at WORD 16 -- the lowest LEGAL location ------
!   p.50: "Pseudo-instruction counters may occupy any of the index
!   registers as well as main core storage locations."  $X0 is word 16.
        Z,   $X1
        LVI, $X0, SUBJA            ! index register 0's value addresses SUBJA
        EXIC, $X0                  ! THE CONTRAST: one word higher, legal
        BAD, $                     ! hang: AD must NOT be set
        SX,  $X1, RESA2            ! the subject DID run
!
! ---- B. the subject at WORD 31 -- the highest illegal location ----
!   "$X15" is index register 15, word 31, half-word address 62.  The
!   counter itself (PSICB) is perfectly legal; only what it points at
!   is not.  That is jb_ax5b TEST 3B's shape exactly: "leaves the
!   pseudo-IC legal and puts the SUBJECT INSTRUCTION below 32".
        Z,   $X1
        LX,  $X4, SUBJW            ! the WORD holding "LVI,$X1,(8)777"
        SX,  $X4, $X15             ! ... planted in index register 15
        LV,  $X5, PSICB            ! the counter BEFORE
        EXIC, PSICB
        BADZ, B1                   ! AD must be SET
        B,   $                     ! hang: AD was not set
B1      KV,  $X1, ZEROW
        BZXE, $                    ! hang: the subject RAN
        KV,  $X5, PSICB
        BZXE, $                    ! hang: the counter was STEPPED anyway --
                                   !   p.50 steps it "after the subject
                                   !   instruction has been fetched", and
                                   !   no subject was fetched
!
! ---- C. the subject at WORD 32 -- the lowest LEGAL location -------
        Z,   $X6
        LV,  $X5, PSICC
        EXIC, PSICC                ! THE CONTRAST: 32.00 exactly
        BAD, $                     ! hang: AD must NOT be set
        SX,  $X6, RESC             ! the subject DID run
        V+I, $X5, 0.32
        KV,  $X5, PSICC
        BZXE, $                    ! hang: ... and the counter stepped once
!
! ---- D. the counter inside the PROTECTED AREA: DS, and suppressed --
!   The set-up runs DISABLED, exactly as programs/storage_protection.
!   strap and jb_ax5b's SIP16 do it: word 2 ($IA) and word 3 (the
!   boundary registers) are themselves protected once the system is
!   enabled.
        LVI, $X13, TBL
        SV,  $X13, $IA             ! the interruption table origin
        LVI, $X13, PROT
        SV,  $X13, $LB             ! lower boundary := PROT
        LVI, $X13, ABOVE
        SV,  $X13, $UB             ! upper boundary := ABOVE
        BBZ, 3.57, $+1.0           ! boundary control := 0, signal INSIDE
        Z,   $X7
        LV,  $X5, PSICD            ! the counter BEFORE
        BE,  $+.32                 ! ENABLE: monitoring is effective now
        EXIC, PSICD                ! PSICD lives inside [PROT, ABOVE)
        BZB, 6.0, $                ! hang if DS's routine did not run
        BD,  $+.32                 ! disable again before the checks
        KV,  $X7, ZEROW
        BZXE, $                    ! hang: the subject RAN
        KV,  $X5, PSICD
        BZXE, $                    ! hang: the counter was STEPPED -- p.32's
                                   !   "storing ... is always suppressed"
!
        B,   PASS
PASS    BEW, $
!
! ---- the subjects -------------------------------------------------
        SLC, (8)7100.0
SUBJA   LX,  $X1, SRC              ! a non-zero mark: "the subject ran"
WRONGA  B,   $                     ! control returned after the SUBJECT
        SLC, (8)7101.0
SUBJW   LX,  $X1, SRC              ! the same half-word, as a WORD to copy
                                   !   into index register 15 (block B)
        SLC, (8)7102.0
SUBJD   LX,  $X7, SRC              ! block D's subject, OUTSIDE the
WRONGD  B,   $                     !   protected area
!
!   Block C's subject lives at 32.00 -- the lowest location p.50 allows
!   a subject of EXIC to occupy, and the whole point of the block.
        SLC, (8)40.0
SUBJC   LX,  $X6, SRC
WRONGC  B,   $
!
! ---- the pseudo-instruction counters ------------------------------
!   Two branch templates per word, never executed; bits 0-18 of the
!   LEFT half-word are the counter, by construction.
        SLC, (8)7200.0
PSICA   B,   SUBJA
        BEW, SUBJA
        SLC, (8)7201.0
PSICB   B,   $X15                  ! -> half-word 62, word 31: ILLEGAL
        BEW, $X15
        SLC, (8)7202.0
PSICC   B,   SUBJC                 ! -> half-word 64, word 32: legal
        BEW, SUBJC
!
! ---- the protected area -------------------------------------------
        SLC, (8)7300.0
PROT    DD(BU,64,8), 0             ! the lower boundary, inclusive
        SLC, (8)7301.0
PSICD   B,   SUBJD                 ! block D's counter, INSIDE the area
        BEW, SUBJD
        SLC, (8)7310.0
ABOVE   DD(BU,64,8), 0             ! the upper boundary, exclusive
!
! ---- data and results ---------------------------------------------
        SLC, (8)7400.0
ZEROW   DD(BU,64,8), 0             ! a zero to compare index values against
        SLC, (8)7401.0
SRC     DD(BU,64,8), (8)123456701234
        SLC, (8)7402.0
RESA    DD(BU,64,8), 0
        SLC, (8)7403.0
RESA2   DD(BU,64,8), 0
        SLC, (8)7404.0
RESC    DD(BU,64,8), 0
!
! ---- the interruption table ---------------------------------------
        SLC, (8)7500.0
TBL     DD(BU,64,8), 0             ! entry N is at TBL + N words
        SLC, (8)7523.0
DSRTN   BB1, 6.0, $+1.0            ! entry 19 = DS: record the violation in
                                   !   a word that can never signal
        END, START
